WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.568 --> 00:00:20.873
Hello and welcome back to in History, the talk show that features famous people from the past.

2
00:00:21.873 --> 00:00:23.613
I'm your host, Faye Moss.

3
00:00:24.534 --> 00:00:32.856
Today's guest was a NASA astronaut who became one of the most famous people on Earth—the first human

4
00:00:33.248 --> 00:00:35.109
to step on the moon!

5
00:00:36.169 --> 00:00:41.390
Coming to us straight from history, here is Neil Armstrong!

6
00:00:59.356 --> 00:00:59.976
Thank you, Faye.

7
00:01:01.308 --> 00:01:08.812
You were born in Ohio in 1930, and from an early age, you demonstrated that you'd go far

8
00:01:08.893 --> 00:01:09.313
in life.

9
00:01:10.193 --> 00:01:14.756
But you went really far—all the way to outer space!

10
00:01:15.676 --> 00:01:18.438
When did your interest in flying begin?

11
00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:23.501
When I was only two years old, my dad took me to see the National Air Races in

12
00:01:23.561 --> 00:01:24.462
Cleveland, Ohio.

13
00:01:25.302 --> 00:01:28.424
Planes raced five laps around a 10-mile loop.

14
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:35.044
Air travel was still relatively new, and it was thrilling to see planes flying around at high speeds.

15
00:01:35.965 --> 00:01:36.525
I was hooked!

16
00:01:38.086 --> 00:01:42.830
For my sixth birthday, my parents gave me a ride on a real plane at an airfield near

17
00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:43.350
our house.

18
00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:46.432
I loved it, even though I felt terrified!

19
00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:51.175
You're not the only famous flier to come from Ohio.

20
00:01:51.976 --> 00:01:57.800
The Wright Brothers, who invented the first successful airplane, were also from your home state.

21
00:01:58.844 --> 00:02:00.605
They were a big inspiration to me.

22
00:02:01.566 --> 00:02:05.728
As a child I spent a lot of time reading books about the Wright Brothers and flying.

23
00:02:06.568 --> 00:02:10.311
I also built model airplanes and tested them to see how well they flew.

24
00:02:11.391 --> 00:02:13.572
Eventually I graduated to real airplanes.

25
00:02:14.493 --> 00:02:17.735
In fact, I flew an airplane before I learned how to drive a car.

26
00:02:18.615 --> 00:02:23.118
Even one of my first jobs—at a bakery—seemed to steer me toward a career in flight.

27
00:02:24.298 --> 00:02:25.579
A job at a bakery?

28
00:02:26.199 --> 00:02:26.860
What do you mean?

29
00:02:27.984 --> 00:02:32.686
Because I was small for my age, it was my job to clean the big vats that mixed

30
00:02:32.766 --> 00:02:35.567
dough. I had to crawl right inside them.

31
00:02:36.548 --> 00:02:39.489
That prepared me for the cramped spaces inside a spacecraft.

32
00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:45.492
Later I started college through a scholarship program that required me to serve in the Navy for a

33
00:02:45.552 --> 00:02:50.554
time. I received more flight training and became a naval pilot in 1950.

34
00:02:51.314 --> 00:02:54.476
By the next year, I was flying fighter jets in the Korean War.

35
00:02:55.536 --> 00:02:59.299
I had a close call over Wonsan and had to eject out of my plane.

36
00:03:00.119 --> 00:03:01.240
Luckily I was rescued.

37
00:03:02.341 --> 00:03:04.242
Lucky for history too!

38
00:03:04.883 --> 00:03:10.887
Eventually you were discharged and you returned to college, graduating in 1955.

39
00:03:11.627 --> 00:03:12.688
What did you do next?

40
00:03:13.729 --> 00:03:19.233
After college I became a test pilot for NASA, and then in 1958, I was selected to be

41
00:03:19.273 --> 00:03:19.973
an astronaut.

42
00:03:20.954 --> 00:03:25.837
At that time NASA was working on the Apollo program, which was a series of missions with the

43
00:03:25.877 --> 00:03:27.879
goal of landing a person on the moon.

44
00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:32.783
In 1969 I was chosen to be the commander for Apollo 11.

45
00:03:33.804 --> 00:03:39.849
That Apollo mission was the first time humans actually landed on the moon.

46
00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:44.393
It made you a true superstar in history!

47
00:03:45.574 --> 00:03:46.214
Please, Faye.

48
00:03:46.494 --> 00:03:47.856
I don't deserve all the credit.

49
00:03:48.696 --> 00:03:52.900
Getting a human on the moon took a lot of people working as a team for years.

50
00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:55.361
It wasn't just the astronauts.

51
00:03:56.522 --> 00:04:02.046
There were scientists, computer programmers, and other hardworking people who made the mission possible.

52
00:04:03.047 --> 00:04:05.869
Not to mention aviation pioneers like the Wright Brothers.

53
00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:10.913
Did you know, I brought some parts from their first aircraft with me to the moon?

54
00:04:11.913 --> 00:04:13.534
That's so cool!

55
00:04:14.375 --> 00:04:19.139
Why don't you tell us a little bit about that first moon landing on July 20, 1969?

56
00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.261
Well, for one thing, we almost didn't make it.

57
00:04:25.061 --> 00:04:30.162
We were about 1,300 feet from the surface of the moon, descending in the lunar module, the Eagle.

58
00:04:31.023 --> 00:04:35.464
We had gone a little fast on the landing and had overshot our planned landing spot.

59
00:04:36.384 --> 00:04:38.845
I looked out the window and saw a field of boulders.

60
00:04:40.005 --> 00:04:43.286
I didn't want to land on a pile of rocks—that could be dangerous.

61
00:04:44.467 --> 00:04:47.948
Oh—and we had only about a minute's worth of fuel left!

62
00:04:49.488 --> 00:04:52.049
How did you ever manage to land?

63
00:04:53.289 --> 00:04:56.250
At 500 feet I took manual control of the Eagle.

64
00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:01.271
I thought we were close enough to the surface that I could land before the fuel ran out.

65
00:05:02.212 --> 00:05:06.033
I slowed the Eagle down and looked for a landing place without big rocks.

66
00:05:07.093 --> 00:05:10.194
We landed with 43 seconds' worth of fuel left.

67
00:05:12.115 --> 00:05:16.156
Whew! That's definitely a high-pressure situation!

68
00:05:17.656 --> 00:05:21.238
It must have been a relief to step out onto the moon.

69
00:05:22.598 --> 00:05:23.038
It was.

70
00:05:23.999 --> 00:05:28.361
Yet there was still some pressure on us because it was such an important moment in history.

71
00:05:29.301 --> 00:05:34.483
When I first stepped on the surface of the moon, I said, "That's one small step for man,

72
00:05:35.084 --> 00:05:37.005
one giant leap for mankind."

73
00:05:38.125 --> 00:05:44.388
I thought I'd said, "That's one small step for a man," but I guess it didn't come out

74
00:05:44.448 --> 00:05:47.705
right! No worries.

75
00:05:48.245 --> 00:05:49.606
We all get your point!

76
00:05:50.527 --> 00:05:55.850
Now, the thing I'm sure everyone wants to know is what was the moon like?

77
00:05:56.911 --> 00:05:58.232
It was very pretty out there.

78
00:05:59.233 --> 00:06:01.394
The surface was fine and powdery.

79
00:06:02.255 --> 00:06:05.717
I stopped to look out at the horizon; it was a magnificent sight.

80
00:06:06.718 --> 00:06:11.321
When you're on the moon, it looks much like a desert, but the moon has a stark beauty

81
00:06:11.441 --> 00:06:12.042
all its own.

82
00:06:13.262 --> 00:06:15.244
I didn't have much time for sightseeing though.

83
00:06:16.316 --> 00:06:20.900
I was out on the moon with another astronaut, Buzz Aldrin, and we spent most of our time

84
00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:23.963
setting up equipment for experiments and collecting samples.

85
00:06:25.164 --> 00:06:27.906
We only had two and a half hours to get all of our work done.

86
00:06:28.847 --> 00:06:36.613
When you returned to Earth, people all over the world wanted to meet you and your fellow astronauts.

87
00:06:36.633 --> 00:06:37.414
They had to wait!

88
00:06:38.294 --> 00:06:41.917
We were quarantined to make sure we hadn't caught any diseases from the moon.

89
00:06:42.958 --> 00:06:48.393
Following our release from quarantine, we were sent on a seven-week goodwill tour of the world where we

90
00:06:48.433 --> 00:06:51.034
met heads of state and other officials and dignitaries.

91
00:06:52.634 --> 00:06:57.095
After that I tried to get back to a normal life; I didn't like being famous.

92
00:06:58.296 --> 00:07:02.297
I never flew in space again and retired from NASA in 1971.

93
00:07:03.357 --> 00:07:07.838
You retired from NASA, but you stayed interested in flying, right?

94
00:07:08.998 --> 00:07:10.479
Of course, Faye!

95
00:07:10.499 --> 00:07:14.480
I became a professor of aerospace engineering at the University of Cincinnati.

96
00:07:15.456 --> 00:07:19.079
This gave me a chance to teach young people and help them reach their dreams.

97
00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:26.505
I know you're a very private person, so thank you for taking time to share your story with

98
00:07:26.645 --> 00:07:28.787
us. My pleasure, Faye!

99
00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:39.476
Neil Armstrong continued to live a mostly quiet life but stayed involved in the aerospace world.

100
00:07:40.512 --> 00:07:48.075
He died in 2012 at the age of 82, having traveled farther than most other humans on Earth.

101
00:07:49.275 --> 00:07:56.677
Streets, schools, buildings, and even a crater and an asteroid have been named in his honor.

102
00:07:57.698 --> 00:07:59.798
I hope you enjoyed my chat with Neil.

103
00:08:00.779 --> 00:08:04.160
See you next time on Superstars in History!
